...It has been about two or three years since I last played Nethack.

The first game, a week ago, started well enough. I played a chaotic monk 
(always my favourite class), started off with a nice little assortment 
of items, found a decent general store on dungeon level 3 or 4 and then 
a co-aligned altar one level further down. And then disaster struck, in 
the form of a particularly nasty ant. dywypi?

Game number two, the one which resulted in this YAAP, was weird.

First off, it felt like every other dungeon level had an altar on it. 
Only one - the one in Minetown - was co-aligned to start with, but they 
all (save for the Aligned ones in Gehennom, of course) ended up firmly 
chaotic, in two cases resulting in the summoning of Demon Lords 
(Yoohooglum, the sack of disease, and a very irritating Asmodeus, both 
of whom I slaughtered as soon as I was able - nothing is quite so 
annoying as setting up camp on a level and then having your every step 
down a narrow corridor blocked by a "peaceful" demon lord).

Then there was Einstein, my little doggie companion. Someplace in the 
gnomish mines, Einstein... changed a bit. Into a very useful grey 
dragon. He survived quite a while, did the (now renamed) Puff the Magic 
Dragon, accompanying me all the way to the basement altar room, where 
the horde of aligned priests finally proved too much for him, the poor 
thing. Still, maybe that's karma for all of those shopkeepers he 
slaughtered on those early levels.

The other weirdness about this game was the number of bones levels. I 
think there were about six in all, including Yoogleflump's home level 
and the Minetown shops. The latter of which resulted in the sad demise 
of Izchak, despite my best efforts. You see, while I do use the "hearse" 
program (to automatically swap bones files on the interweb, in case you 
don't know the program), that particular one was one of mine, probably 
from 3-4 years ago. Moreover, it was one where Izchak had killed my 
earlier incarnation. And, since we share a player name, he and I, the 
poor chap was still mad at me when I showed up in this game.

Oh, I tried to placate him with cash. Really I did. But he wasn't having 
any of it. And in the end I had to run away as fast as my little speed 
boots could carry me, leaving Puff behind to do the dirty work.

Other oddities: Sokoban tower was awkward, since I had to work the 
puzzle out from scratch (because it's been so long since I've played 
Nethack). I managed to fight my way all the way up to the top, though. 
Only to find *no* bag of holding. Nothing.

As best as I can figure it, one of the zoo residents must have eaten it 
before I got there, as the cupboards were all open. But since the 
battles were half me and half Puff, I don't know what ate it - there 
were no incriminating corpses at any rate. I didn't find a bag of 
holding until a couple of levels before Medusa, where a handy bones pile 
furnished one. It went: Level 21: Altar, Level 22: Bones pile with bag 
of holding, Level 24: Medusa. Which was handy, as previously it had been 
a right pain moving base camp without a BoH.

Around ten levels down, there was a cockatrice nest, which furnished me 
with twelve eggs. Not being a complete idiot, I figured that those eggs 
were probably related to the cockatrices in the nest and so, rather than 
munching on them, I waited patiently until I managed to scare up some 
spare Identify scrolls, whereupon I found that I was right: I had a nice 
batch of useful stoning grenades.

Even so, I was patient, saving them for Rodney instead of wasting them, 
willy-nilly, on lesser enemies. Which proved to be a good idea, because 
Rodney showed up NINE times altogether, but I never did have to fight 
him, as those 12 grenades turned out to be just enough. Well, *only* 
just, since I twice swiped him with a handy cockatrice he'd summoned 
himself. Oh, the irony.

I was getting a bit concerned at one point, thinking I'd run out of 
cockatrice eggs, and so I tried polymorphing myself into a cockatrice 
and laying a few eggs (incidentally, a polymorph trap had changed my 
gender at some point - I never did change it back to male, so this is my 
one and only ascension as a "Demigoddess"). This was on my way back up 
out of the dungeon. However, it turned out that my eggs just hatched 
*really* quickly, and so I abandoned that idea. Luckily, I didn't run 
out of eggs after all.

So, with all of those bones piles, plus judicious ploypiling, I ended up 
with several wands of wishing. 16 wishes in all. Though, frankly, every 
one of those wishes was completely wasted on trivial (but vital at the 
time) items. For example, I know I wished for about 12 potions of full 
healing (just in case - I ended up using five of them, though, so just 
as well I did. On the other hand, I - again, from bones - ended the game 
with seven unused amulets of life saving, so meh) just before I entered 
the elemental plains. And I also wished for several cursed scrolls of 
gold detection at the same time.

The only really "vital" wish in the game, though, was on the Plain of 
Air, where I suddenly discovered that I didn't have a wand of opening, 
for to escape from the innards of an elemental which was battering me 
something rotten. Even that one was hardly vital, however, as it turned 
out, since I ended up just using the wands of death I'd accumulated 
along the way far more often than I used that wand of opening.

Once on the Astral Plain, I got lucky: I headed to the right, wand of 
deathed the minor players, slaughtered assorted heavenly guardians and 
managed to kill Pestilence (with the assistance of my trusty blessed +6 
unicorn horn) then run for the altar. Which, miracle of miracles, turned 
out to be the chaotic altar, as so fervently desired. So I never had to 
tangle with Death at all, just sacrificed the old amulet and ascended 
like Billy-Oh.

Though *technically* I did eat one brown pudding (thinking it counted as 
a vegetable, much like oozes do) and I did accidentally side-swipe a 
xorn with a pickaxe on the plains of earth, *morally* I'm asserting that 
I did not hit with a wielded weapon and that I ate all vegetarian (aside 
from that pudding nibble). Frankly, I'd like to be able to set an option 
to give me a warning if some action is about to violate such conducts - 
especially since it's so easy to accidentally hit something with a 
pick-axe and the only way to *know* which foods are "monk safe" are to 
read a spoiler. And if I can't remember which foods are safe for monks 
to eat (given that I *wrote* that spoiler myself, several years ago) 
then...

Anyways, *officially* the only voluntary challenge I stuck to was "You 
did not wish for any artifacts." I did end up with several as god-gifts 
and from bones levels, though. So it's no all bad.

Here endeth the lesson.


